\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A doublet and two trivial lines}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In the finite group of \ref{fdip-0126}, write #{2T} for the
#{24} Hurwitz units and #{\gamma=(1+\ui\sqrt{3})/2}. Represent
a quaternion #{u} by left multiplication #{M(u)} on the
quaternions regarded as a right complex space of dimension
two. Associativity makes this action complex-linear and
multiplicative, and a unit quaternion preserves the norm.
On #{2T\times\langle\gamma\rangle}, take the doublet
#{(u,\gamma^m)\mapsto\gamma^m M(u)}. Since #{\gamma^3=-1}
and #{M(-1)=-\bbI}, the central pair #{(-1,\gamma^3)} acts
by the identity. This gives a representation of the quotient
#{\Gew=(2T\times\langle\gamma\rangle)/\langle(-1,\gamma^3)\rangle}.}

\p{Use this doublet as #{V_+} and two trivial complex lines
as #{V_-}. The class #{z} of #{(-1,1)} is distinct from
the identity since the subgroup divided out contains just
#{(1,1)} and #{(-1,\gamma^3)}. At #{z} the traces are
#{\trace(-\bbI_2)=-2} and #{\trace(\bbI_2)=2}.
Conjugation fixes both numbers. More directly, any intertwiner
#{b:V_+\to V_-} must satisfy #{b(-v)=b(v)}. Additivity forces
#{b=0}. Thus \ref{fdip-018D} excludes both kinds of exchange
for these actions with the identity group identification. The zero
endomorphism on #{V_+\oplus V_-} has this whole graded space as
its kernel. Its [index](fdip-013N) is #{2-2=0} even though
neither equivariant exchange exists.}

\p{For the trace test, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{LinearEquiv.trace_eq_map_trace} gives
#{\trace(T')=\sigma(\trace(T))} when a #{\sigma}-semilinear
equivalence #{b} satisfies #{T'b=bT} on finite-dimensional
complex spaces, for #{\sigma} the identity or conjugation.
Here #{T} and #{T'} are the two actions of #{z}.}
